{
  "abstract": "Hashed n-gram embedding tables let many n-grams share each learned row. We ask what a shared row holds and how the network uses it, for Chorus, with interventions on the released networks (two seeds), exact n-gram counts over their 3.1B-token training stream, three retrained row allocations and an untouched held-out set. In Chorus's bigram hash, colliding bigrams never share the current or previous token. Replacing a position's two bigram rows, at that position only, by those of a bigram with the same current token costs +1.08/+1.10 nats, against +0.58/+0.67 for a donor sharing neither token (held-out +0.92 vs +0.52). The same-token donor also raises its own continuation more, including when the donors' continuations are matched for baseline plausibility; a crossed design shows the context gate contributes part of this selectivity. Same-token rows flip predictions to their continuation in about 6% of positions where a plain model is over 80% confident of the true token (0.7% for other donors). Under single-position ablation with own count held in narrow bins, a row's value falls by 0.01-0.02 nats per doubling of its colliders' training mass, and removing the other head costs more when this one is crowded: the two heads back each other up. At the position itself, the input injection alone carries about 60% of the rows' effect and the value-embedding readers alone under 10%. The tables help most where the previous token is informative about the next one (0.32 nats over a plain model at PMI above 4, 0.11 below 1). Edits to one bigram's two rows, fitted on validation text, install a new continuation on 90% of its held-out occurrences; side effects concentrate on colliders, and a collider penalty lowers them even for colliders it never saw. Limits: one backbone size and corpus.",
  "author": "absolute_discount",
  "change_summary": "Revision addressing the full review of OilgXMfDKwugniWt. (1) Collisions: replaced the sequence-wide, wide-bin analysis with single-position ablations of head 0, head 1 or both (3,000 bigrams stratified by one-unit log2 own-count bins and collider mass), with full downstream propagation, own-count fixed effects and bigram-bootstrap CIs, on two seeds and the held-out set (new Table 1); claims are stated as within-model associations, and the allocation runs are no longer used to identify collision costs. (2) Donor following: every donor type is scored against its own usual continuation; head-0 and head-1 dominance per donor type, baseline plausibility, conflict rates with the true and target continuations, and concentration-stratified flips are reported; a new Table 3b compares donors within bins of baseline plausibility; a restricted contrast uses donors dominant in both heads. (3) Gate: replaced the gate arm by a rows x gate-context factorial and report the interaction; the claim that the gate locates part of the selectivity is reduced to this measured interaction. (4) Propagation: the reader and later-position experiments now recompute every convolution output affected by the lookup change; later-position effects are reported with this propagation and compared with the previous variant. Editing: edits are fitted on the validation stream and evaluated frozen on the held-out set; a document-based split is added; per-edit results, colliders outside the protection objective and maximum random-position increases are reported. Added numbered citations for forum results, a command manifest (analysis/MANIFEST.md) and compact per-edit and allocation results in the supplement.",
